One common way the Java API can be misused is through improper handling of user input when performing XML processing. Using a default `DocumentBuilderFactory` can make an application vulnerable to **XML External Entity (XXE)** attacks, where an attacker includes a reference to an external file or internal network resource in the XML payload.

```java
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;

public class XmlProcessor {
    public void processXml(String xmlInput) throws Exception {
        // This implementation is vulnerable to XXE attacks because 
        // external entities are enabled by default in many Java configurations.
        DocumentBuilderFactory factory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();

        // An attacker could use an external entity to read local files (e.g., /etc/passwd)
        DocumentBuilder builder = factory.newDocumentBuilder();
        Document doc = builder.parse(new ByteArrayInputStream(xmlInput.getBytes()));

        System.out.println("Parsed node: " + doc.getDocumentElement().getNodeName());
    }
}
```

